{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790428574,"nonce":"Q6INFbrBRFdvJSYWf5XG53QYyfI=","message":"Direction (2026-09-26, user): no interpreter is embedded on the wasm target. WrenLift is taking Ash's wasm codegen approach, so Wren modules compile to wasm and a custom wasm linker, like Ash's ash_wasm_link, puts both runtimes' modules into one. This replaces the part of this issue's text that had Wren's interpreter joining Ash's prelinked runtime object. How Wren fibers suspend on wasm is decided by WrenLift's codegen: host suspension, or its state-machine transform. Every language that ships to wasm needs a compiled path; Zyntax's is filed separately.","files":null}]}